{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788935477,"nonce":"ReLdxU21/HXgBLJpPlDUCuKbmPM=","message":"CODE LANDED, BENCH ACCEPTANCE NOT DISCHARGED. Implemented overnight 2026-09-09,\nmerged to main as 1ec3e5b, rebuilt at 562f553.\n\nPart 1: EnclosureReport.Gaps is now List\u003cobject\u003e, matching RoofHoles and Rooms\ntwo lines below it in the same class. Fixed at the DECLARATION rather than\nconverted at Out(), so the type reaching the envelope is representable and there\nis no conversion left for the next author to forget.\n\nPart 2: THE LOUD DEFAULT, and the general IEnumerable arm is refused. The reason\nis architectural rather than aesthetic. MiniJson.Write runs on the poller thread\n— Runtime.Result.Data is serialized off-thread — so enumerating whatever it is\nhanded would be Verse access from the file half of the bridge, which CLAUDE.md\nforbids outright. Verse enumerables are not all inert reads: some traverse\nregions or rebuild a cache on iteration, and one mutated mid-enumeration throws.\nThat would end \"the writer never throws\" in the one place that owes every\nconsumed command exactly one result file. ToString() on the default arm is one\nalready-wrapped call; enumeration is an unbounded amount of game code. The\ndefault arm now calls Journal.EmitWarning naming the offending type and the key\nbefore it writes the string; EmitWarning already dedupes on exact text and has an\noverflow cap, so it is one line per (type, key) per session.\n\nPart 3: DesignateReach's hand-conversion STAYS, now commented as load-bearing\nrather than vestigial. Removing it was only ever conditional on taking the\ngeneral case.\n\nPart 4: MiniJson's header states what the writer can and cannot represent, in\nfull, plus why there is no general arm.\n\nREAL EXECUTION EVIDENCE, without the game. MiniJson.cs was compiled verbatim into\na scratch console project alongside the shipped version and both were run over a\n36-value corpus: 36 checks, 0 failures. The bug reproduced exactly and the fix\nproduced the array. Output is BIT-IDENTICAL old-vs-new on all 36 values —\nscalars, escapes, NaN and Infinity, nested dict and list, string[] and\nList\u003cstring\u003e and HashSet\u003cstring\u003e, boxed nullables, the depth guard, a\nself-referential list and dict, and an object whose ToString() throws. Parse\nreads gaps back as a 2-element array whose [0] has the same key set as first_gap.\n\nThe issue's survey holds. Widened past it: Dictionary\u003cstring,int\u003e is the\nidentical trap and has three sites — Journal.CountsSince, DigestVerb.kinds,\nThingVerbs.Stuffs — all three already converted at the envelope. A scan of the 77\ncaptured envelopes under RUNS/ found no other ToString() leakage, so the loud\ndefault should be a quiet canary on today's code rather than a noise source.\n\nWORTH KNOWING: accept/a1644d6-enclosure.py already asserts gaps is a list, in\nfive checks (1.3g, 2.4a-e, 5.4a, 5.5). Every one of them fails against the\nshipped string. That suite appears never to have been run green, which is\nconsistent with this issue's note that only the detection half was verified.\n\nSTILL OWED: the in-game acceptance, unchanged — place a room layout, remove one\nshell cell, read construction {layout_id}, assert gaps is a JSON array whose\nfirst element matches first_gap. The suite for it needs no change.","files":null}]}